1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,141
Hi, I'm Somama Siddiqui.

2
00:00:02,141 --> 00:00:08,958
My project asks a simple question: can a webpage trick an AI agent into sending an email to the wrong person?

3
00:00:08,958 --> 00:00:14,131
The agent has a legitimate job, but the page it reads contains instructions from an attacker.

4
00:00:14,131 --> 00:00:19,594
I'll walk you through my system, show you the actual saved results, and explain what still needs work.

5
00:00:19,594 --> 00:00:24,999
These examples replay recorded experiments rather than running new API calls.

6
00:00:25,383 --> 00:00:32,995
For my experiment, I give the agent one task: read a solar-array article, summarize it, and email the professor.

7
00:00:32,995 --> 00:00:37,266
All three tools are local simulations, so no real email is sent.

8
00:00:37,266 --> 00:00:44,430
My benchmark has twenty-four attack designs and six conditions, including a baseline and five defense configurations.

9
00:00:44,430 --> 00:00:48,851
Each proposed action goes through the parser and defense checks before it can execute.

10
00:00:48,851 --> 00:00:52,347
I save the tool calls and outcomes so the results can be checked.

11
00:00:52,716 --> 00:00:55,722
Let me show you an actual direct-override test.

12
00:00:55,722 --> 00:00:59,391
The malicious page tells the agent to email the attacker.

13
00:00:59,391 --> 00:01:05,016
But in the saved log, you can see two page reads, a summary, and an email to the professor.

14
00:01:05,016 --> 00:01:07,388
The agent then finishes normally.

15
00:01:07,388 --> 00:01:11,150
In this case, the attack failed and the legitimate task succeeded.

16
00:01:11,150 --> 00:01:16,891
I treat that as one observed result, not proof that the baseline is secure against every attack.

17
00:01:17,258 --> 00:01:21,120
This next example shows why I measure usefulness as well as security.

18
00:01:21,120 --> 00:01:26,641
Under instruction-hierarchy prompting, the agent reads the role-play attack and refuses.

19
00:01:26,641 --> 00:01:31,143
It doesn't email the attacker, but it doesn't complete the professor's task either.

20
00:01:31,143 --> 00:01:38,226
In another episode, the correct summary is sent, but the agent reaches the six-step limit without a final response.

21
00:01:38,226 --> 00:01:40,344
I keep that record marked as an error.

22
00:01:40,344 --> 00:01:44,222
I don't count it as a completed run just because one useful action happened.

23
00:01:44,591 --> 00:01:46,937
Here's my current progress on the Groq pilot.

24
00:01:46,937 --> 00:01:52,967
Of thirty planned episodes, sixteen are valid, one has an error, and thirteen haven't been attempted.

25
00:01:52,967 --> 00:01:54,900
All six clean controls passed.

26
00:01:54,900 --> 00:02:01,011
Among the ten valid attack tests, no attack succeeded, and nine completed the legitimate task.

27
00:02:01,011 --> 00:02:04,321
These tests cover direct override and part of role-play.

28
00:02:04,321 --> 00:02:10,710
I still need the other attack categories and repeated trials before I can make broader claims or rank the defenses.

29
00:02:11,091 --> 00:02:19,976
Since the midterm, I've extended the project with matched-trial analysis, coverage checks, a Groq adapter, and an AI-assisted review workflow.

30
00:02:19,976 --> 00:02:25,729
This code converts the model's tool request into the action format that my agent already checks locally.

31
00:02:25,729 --> 00:02:29,953
A fresh software test run passed all fifty-six tests.

32
00:02:29,953 --> 00:02:34,907
I also used AI assistance to audit twenty-four earlier Gemini records.

33
00:02:34,907 --> 00:02:41,759
That audit was unblinded, so I report it as AI-assisted review, not independent human validation.

34
00:02:42,133 --> 00:02:46,388
My next step is to investigate the termination errors and finish the pilot.

35
00:02:46,388 --> 00:02:52,696
Then I'll move to the full grid: one hundred fifty episodes for one trial, or four hundred fifty for three.

36
00:02:52,696 --> 00:02:56,585
I also need to review thirty to fifty transcripts from the new study.

37
00:02:56,585 --> 00:03:03,101
My target is eighty to eighty-five percent completion, but the saved evidence doesn't support claiming that yet.

38
00:03:03,101 --> 00:03:08,831
My report and repository make both the completed work and the remaining gaps clear.

39
00:03:08,831 --> 00:03:09,594
Thank you.
